1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,141
Can you show me health checks:

2
00:00:07,141 --> 00:00:10,184
liveness, readiness and startup probes?

3
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:16,477
A probe is a check the kubelet runs against a container, again and again:

4
00:00:16,477 --> 00:00:20,175
an HTTP request, a TCP connection or a command.

5
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:24,738
Kubernetes has three, each answering a different question:

6
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,549
"The process runs" is not "the application works".

7
00:00:28,549 --> 00:00:32,058
A web server can be running while it is still loading,

8
00:00:32,058 --> 00:00:36,606
while its database is unreachable, or while it is stuck in a deadlock.

9
00:00:36,606 --> 00:00:42,390
Without probes Kubernetes sends traffic to all of these and never restarts the stuck one.

10
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:46,553
And how does it actually work?

11
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:52,003
The kubelet on the Pod's node runs each probe every periodSeconds.

12
00:00:52,003 --> 00:00:55,049
After failureThreshold failures in a row:

13
00:00:55,049 --> 00:01:11,911
| Probe fails | What Kubernetes does | Typical endpoint | |---|---|---| | startup | keeps waiting; after failureThreshold × periodSeconds it restarts the container | the same as liveness | | readiness | marks the Pod not Ready:

14
00:01:11,911 --> 00:01:30,408
the Service stops sending it traffic; the Pod keeps running | checks dependencies (database) | | liveness | restarts the container (the restart counter goes up) | checks only the process itself | An HTTP probe succeeds on any status from 200 to 399.

15
00:01:30,408 --> 00:01:33,677
Our backend has two endpoints made for this:

16
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:40,437
/livez (always 200 while the process runs) and /readyz (200 only when its database answers,

17
00:01:40,437 --> 00:01:41,997
or when it has none).

18
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:43,037
Rule of thumb:

19
00:01:43,037 --> 00:01:46,379
liveness must never depend on other services.

20
00:01:48,133 --> 00:01:48,300
1.

21
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:51,963
Deploy, and look at the Pod while it starts:

22
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,306
Running but 0/1 ready:

23
00:01:54,306 --> 00:02:01,365
the container runs, the startup probe has not succeeded yet (the backend sleeps 15 s).

24
00:02:01,365 --> 00:02:01,529
2.

25
00:02:01,529 --> 00:02:03,417
Wait until it is ready:

26
00:02:03,417 --> 00:02:03,581
3.

27
00:02:03,581 --> 00:02:05,879
Traffic through the Service:

28
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:06,599
3.

29
00:02:06,599 --> 00:02:08,923
Traffic through the Service:

30
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:13,576
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

31
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:19,198
The probes as Kubernetes understood them, and the Pod's readiness condition:

32
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:22,607
Let me try something.

33
00:02:22,607 --> 00:02:30,973
Tell the backend to use a database that does not exist (lesson 12's variables, set with kubectl set env):

34
00:02:31,533 --> 00:02:33,469
What should happen?

35
00:02:33,469 --> 00:02:35,608
The update rolls out.

36
00:02:35,608 --> 00:02:37,034
What happened?

37
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:38,868
It never finishes.

38
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:45,588
Roll back to the previous version of the Deployment (lesson 09):

39
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:48,047
Got it.

40
00:02:48,047 --> 00:02:50,798
What do I need to remember?

41
00:02:51,333 --> 00:02:52,173
Startup:

42
00:02:52,173 --> 00:02:54,061
finished starting?

43
00:02:54,061 --> 00:02:55,110
Readiness:

44
00:02:55,110 --> 00:02:57,103
may it get traffic?

45
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:04,506
A failing readiness probe removes the Pod from the Service and stalls a rolling update, protecting users.

46
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:12,151
Liveness checks only the process; a liveness probe that depends on other services causes restart storms.
